1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,966
Your backtest and your live system are usually two different codebases.

2
00:00:04,066 --> 00:00:05,641
That's why they drift.

3
00:00:05,983 --> 00:00:08,220
You describe it once, in Rust.

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:13,216
For example: an order book, fanned out to each side, recombined into a quote.

5
00:00:14,017 --> 00:00:15,975
Under the hood it's a graph.

6
00:00:16,075 --> 00:00:20,193
Each node runs once per cycle, even where branches recombine.

7
00:00:20,293 --> 00:00:22,774
About twenty-seven nanoseconds each.

8
00:00:23,283 --> 00:00:26,733
Historically: an instant, deterministic replay.

9
00:00:26,783 --> 00:00:28,800
Run the same graph live.

10
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,638
Paced by the wall clock.

11
00:00:30,738 --> 00:00:32,649
Same values, same order.

12
00:00:32,749 --> 00:00:34,700
Only the clock changes.

13
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:36,383
One definition.

14
00:00:36,483 --> 00:00:38,221
Two run modes.

15
00:00:38,321 --> 00:00:40,663
The implementations can't drift.

16
00:00:41,017 --> 00:00:42,487
Wingfoil.

17
00:00:42,587 --> 00:00:44,798
Stream processing in Rust.
